year="2022",
publisher="Springer International Publishing",
address="Cham",
pages="99--114",
abstract="The statistical theory of turbulence, perceived as a theory of correlation functions between fluctuating quantities, began in the early 1920s with Taylor's analysis of the ``diffusing power'' of turbulence and reached a climax in the 1930s when hot-wire measurements of grid-generated wind tunnel turbulence enabled a comparison of theory with experimental data. The statistical concept at this stage was mainly developed by Taylor in Cambridge and von K{\'a}rm{\'a}n in Pasadena; the crucial measurements were made in wind tunnels in England (Simmons, Salter), Germany (Prandtl, Reichardt) and the USA (Dryden and collaborators). In 1939, Kolmogorov started another research effort with the focus on the smallest scale of turbulence for which he postulated universal features. This theory was published in 1941, but remained unknown outside Russia until the end of the Second World War.",
isbn="978-3-030-91459-2",
doi="10.1007/978-3-030-91459-2_7",
